# Every text file checks out with LF on every host. A Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf on would otherwise turn it into CRLF, which breaks shebang
# lines, multi-line string literals and a fixture a test reads byte for byte.
# A repository with no Windows job yet carries the rule too: it costs nothing
# elsewhere, and a pipeline gains a Windows job without a checkout changing.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts keep LF endings on every platform: a CRLF shebang line makes the
# interpreter fail to start. The rule above says as much for every text file;
# this one also holds a script git would not detect as text.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Formats this repository commits as binary, declared rather than inferred.
#
# Git guesses binary from content, and what it guesses on is a NUL byte, so
# without these lines "is this file binary?" and "does this file contain a NUL?"
# are the same question and a gate cannot use one to judge the other.
# ci/gates/no-nul-bytes.py reads these declarations for that reason.
#
# Declaring them also stops git trying to diff or merge them as text, and,
# coming after the first rule, turns its `text=auto` back into `-text` for each.
# A new binary format needs a line here; a file that needs a NUL and is NOT one
# of these formats almost certainly wants an escape instead. The first block is
# the media the project's repositories commit, the second the images, archives
# and fonts.
*.wasm binary
*.glb  binary
*.webm binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.mid  binary

*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.jar  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
